I'm pleased to welcome back to the  program dr. Christopher De Carlo who's  an award-winning philosopher educator an  author of his latest book on critical  thinking called six steps to better  thinking how to disagree and get along  it's great to have you back you know  this is a topic that's very very  interesting to me and we've talked a lot  about sort of the discoveries that  behavioral economics has made over the  last several decades that oftentimes  when you present people you're arguing  with with sort of counter contradictory  information we would like to imagine  that they're going to see the light and  sort of change their mind but very very  often this is not actually the way it  goes and you talk in the book about how  to have some of those conversations and  not only get along but maybe be more  effective so let's start there what's a  good general principle to think about  when you're debating something like  politics with other people that disagree  right to me the first thing you have to  do is be aware of your own biases and  why you've arrived at your current  position and then to be able to wonder  why that other person is thinking  differently from you and what biases  might have led them to that same uh you  know opposing viewpoint and when you do  that the goal is what or I guess is your  goal or should your goal be to change  minds what would be the best approach  empirically speaking to have the best  shot at doing that right so if the art  of argumentation is trying to persuade  somebody of your particular position and  you're better aware of a your own biases  within the context of what the other  person's biases might be this goes a  long way towards trying to find some  type of medium point between where  you're both coming down and so it  becomes easier to try to reach out to  that person with whom you're disagreeing  and be able to communicate more clearly  and more effectively that's interesting  because in some cases the sword of truth  might be a middle ground between  two positions but very often that's not  the case right and I find that that's a  sort of a starting point for a lot of  people who say well listen this is the  position of one side and this is the  position of the other side and more than  likely we all have our biases and the  truth is sort of somewhere in the middle  but a lot of times that that's really  not the case right like the truth might  be the view that is typically subscribe  to by a political party or a particular  movement or an institution or whatever  the case may be so how do you deal with  that how do you deal with the reality  that very often the truth isn't just the  sort of average of all the positions  that are out there right so there are a  number of techniques that can be used in  cases like that you can use the Socratic  approach which I personally like where  in order to try to get somebody who has  opposing views about a position that you  you know and all perspectives is simply  wrong you can get them to talk through  it and what Socrates did is he started  off by agreeing with the person so it's  kind of like well let's assume that  you're right this is known as a reductio  ad absurdum let's assume that you're  right and let's follow along where your  propositions where your premise is Lee  and if you can try to get the person to  walk through their particular  understanding of a topic they themselves  might be able to see where it is they've  made you know a fallacy or a fallacious  form of reasoning so that's the Socratic  form another form is you try to find  shared values with the other person and  then try to appreciate coming at it from  the point of view of a shared value can  they can they see any way in which they  might question what they currently  believe and and better understand why it  is you hold your particular opinion so  those are two techniques that I use  what's your opinion of III I don't know  where this idea started and I don't  think it's original to Peter Bogosian  but he's popularized the DES feasibility  question right which is what evidence if  I could present you  with it would change your mind as sort  of a starting point to getting people to  think wait if the answer is there's no  evidence that would change my mind maybe  I'm not actually basing my opinion on  the evidence what's your opinion of that  strategy right it's it's not a bad  strategy but it immediately confronts a  person and what happens if you're  talking about cherished beliefs about  hot-button issues and a person already  has an enormous amount of emotional  baggage that they're bringing to that  that topic asking them that question  might not have much of an impact on them  if they've already invested so much  emotional activity into basically  maintaining that position so it could  have some efficacy in terms of getting  them to try to understand that if  they're not willing to consider the  possibility of being in error in any way  then then they have to admit that they  are dogmatist well all you've done is  really basically pointed out an obvious  fact that in case of say religious  belief or whatnot you're you really just  calling attention to the fact that  they're just going to say yeah and the  reason why I'm dogmatic is because I'm  right and I'm certain and you're the one  who has issue so sometimes it can help  but I don't know if it if it always will  be productive many of us have been  involved in political disagreements or  discussions where you can sort of sense  it and it's hard to say exactly when it  crosses the line but at a certain point  it gets tense and sometimes you notice  that you might be starting to get a  little hot in the underarms or there's  just something that you sort of sense  that the conversation has crossed from  sort of a friendly conversation into one  where people are starting to get upset  in your experience what causes it to  cross that line yeah I mean you really  kind of talking about you know elements  of randomness and  chaos where things can occur quite  unpredictably in terms of what might set  people off what might cause that kind of  you know emotional turmoil but not and  not so much specifically but what type  of shift in the conversation is what  usually gets to those discussions that  no longer feel okay oftentimes it's a  personal account so if you've struck a  nerve with somebody who has had very  personal experiences with whatever that  topic yet right if it's uh you're  talking about abortion gun control  euthanasia any any major topics the  aspect of politics  if a person has felt that within their  lives and now that somehow brought up  now that's often a case where you're  going to see somebody you know take a  bit of a turn towards more kind of  serious emotional issue or slant towards  the issue in your opinion are the  debates about religion and debates about  politics are they more similar or  different and in other words we could  say well they both relate to sort of  dogmas that one might subscribe to they  both relate to you know ideas that  people are emotionally invested in but  are they really similar types of  disagreement or as religion sort of its  own thing the difference with politics  and religion they're not always separate  but with with religion you have to  understand every time a very deeply  religious person gets up in the morning  they have in their minds the belief that  the information that they're in  possession of is reflective of what is  absolutely true about the nature of the  universe that's no small amount of  information and with politics that this  can vary you know a young man begins as  a liberal and you know enters middle age  and an old age as a conservative with a  with religion that's totally different  story you're talking about the fabric of  reality now you're talking about  something so strong within a person's  it's essentially there is no drug in  comparison with religious conviction I  mean that gets a person higher than any  drug could possibly get them and if  you're going to try to argue against  what it is that gets him that gets them  that high it's like taking the needle  from from the heroin addict or the  bottle from the alcoholic they're gone  often going to fight you on that because  this is what provides them with that  comfort you know feeling what I call  mimetic equilibrium where the world  makes sense the universe makes sense and  all of a sudden you you want to enter  into their worldview doubts and you know  the capacity for error and being wrong  or being misguided that's extremely  deleterious and harmful to their  particular worldview so that's how it  differs from from politics religion what  are the six steps to better thinking  that you outline in the book to dig into  that material a little bit more sure  very easy to remember the six steps  match the first six letters of the  alphabet so eight is for argument people  have to know how to formulate their  ideas into the form of an argument so  that they're better understood B is for  bias we have to understand why it is we  believe what we do and what led us to  this particular point in time  C is for context information doesn't  exist in a vacuum it's always embedded  within some particular type of context  ideas for diagram people and learn a  skill in which when they hear or see or  read what a person is saying there are  skills there are tools that allow them  to literally draw out what that argument  looks like so they can see it on paper  he is for evidence if you're going to  support your argument with evidence  there are various different types f is  for fallacies there's a lot of different  types of policies which are errors and  reasoning so this capacity allows us to  better identify when somebody is making  an error and reasoning and now we can  put a name to the face so to speak can  everybody learn this  and and if so is there sort of some  natural inclination to either be  comfortable in these engaging in these  types of disagreements discussions and  debates because I get a lot of people  who tell me listen I I sort of when I'm  on my own I like thinking about these  things and sort of thinking about what  my positions are on on whatever issue  but I am just not able to engage in a  back-and-forth with someone else and  it's not a question of knowledge of  facts it's just I  constitutionally I freeze up I can't do  it it's not for me do you think these  are skills anybody can learn of course  and their skills of empowerment I mean  I've been lobbying the Ontario  government here mm-hmm  easily the last five years and I've made  some headway with a pilot project with  one of the school school boards here in  Ontario to introduce critical thinking  skills into the curriculum or grades 9  and 10 so that if they can learn them at  those grades and then carry them forward  and grades 11 and 12 they will offer  them levels of empowerment where they  have the capacity now be able to better  understand why it is they believe what  they do so you know I've also visited  other countries as well throughout the  world I have a not-for-profit called a  critical thinking project and what we do  is we do we travel around the world or  we Skype around the world and we teach  teachers the basics of critical thinking  so that in other countries outside of  our own teachers and students can learn  these critical thinking skills and then  the plan is allow them to solve their  problems their way by using these  particular skills the book is six steps  to better thinking how to disagree and  get along we've been speaking with the  books author dr. Christopher de Carlo  thanks again for talking to me about  this and glad to have you back on the  program my pleasure at a time

